    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-94598",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}, & 0\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 1, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{2}}{25},\\,0\\le m\\le 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{2}}{25}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $0\\le m\\le 5.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
